.\" tfidf(1) manual page
.TH TFIDF 1 "2026-07-27" "tfidf-rs 0.0.3" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
tfidf \- composable TF-IDF document indexing and similarity search

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tfidf
.B save
.RB [ \-\-index
.IR PATH ]
.RI [ PATH ...]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.PP
.B tfidf
.B save
.I INDEX
.RI [ PATH ...]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.PP
.B tfidf
.B query
.RB [ \-\-index
.IR PATH ]
.B list
.PP
.B tfidf
.B query
.RB [ \-\-index
.IR PATH ]
.B terms
.RB [ \-\-top
.IR N ]
.PP
.B tfidf
.B query
.RB [ \-\-index
.IR PATH ]
.B similar
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-top
.IR N ]
.PP
.B tfidf
.B query
.RB [ \-\-index
.IR PATH ]
.B search
.RI [ QUERY ]
.RB [ \-\-top
.IR N ]
.PP
.B tfidf
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tfidf
builds a serialized TF-IDF index from files and directories and queries that
index for document paths, aggregate term scores, similar documents, or search
results.
.PP
The program is intended for shell composition. Query output has no headers,
progress indicators, or decorative formatting. Output containing scores is
tab-delimited and can be processed with tools such as
.BR cut (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR xargs (1),
and
.BR head (1).
.PP
Broken output pipes are treated as successful termination. A downstream command
such as
.BR head (1)
may therefore close the pipe without causing a broken-pipe diagnostic.
.PP
Directories passed to
.B save
are walked recursively. Regular files are tokenized and represented as sparse
term-frequency maps. Files that cannot be read and files that produce no tokens
are skipped.

.SH COMMANDS

.SS save
.B tfidf save
builds an index from the supplied files and directories and writes it to the
resolved index path.
.PP
If an input path is a directory, regular files below it are discovered
recursively. An error while reading a directory is fatal. A regular file that
cannot be read is silently skipped.
.PP
Paths that are neither regular files nor directories, including nonexistent
paths, are ignored. If arguments were supplied but none produce indexable
documents, an empty index may be written.
.PP
The parent directory of the output index is created automatically when needed.
An existing index file is replaced. The update is not incremental and is not
performed as an atomic file replacement.
.PP
If
.B \-\-index
is supplied, its argument is the output index path. Otherwise the path is
selected according to
.BR "INDEX PATH RESOLUTION" .
.PP
If
.B \-\-
appears, additional input paths are read from standard input, one path per
line. Leading and trailing whitespace is removed and empty lines are ignored.
Command-line arguments following the first
.B \-\-
are not processed as paths.
.PP
For compatibility, the following positional form is also accepted:
.PP
.RS
.B tfidf save
.I INDEX
.RI [ PATH ...]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RE
.PP
The first positional argument is treated as a legacy index path when at least
one more positional argument is present and one of the following is true:
.IP \(bu 2
its basename is
.B index
or
.BR tfidf.index ;
.IP \(bu 2
its extension is
.BR .index ;
.IP \(bu 2
the path does not currently exist.
.PP
This heuristic is ambiguous. For example, in:
.PP
.RS
.B tfidf save new-file existing-file
.RE
.PP
if
.B new-file
does not exist, it is treated as the output index rather than an input path.
Use
.B \-\-index
to avoid ambiguity.

.SS query list
Print every document path stored in the index, one path per line.
.PP
Output format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PATH
.fi
.RE

.SS query terms
Print the top terms by aggregate TF-IDF score across all indexed documents.
.PP
The default result count is 20.
.PP
Output format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
TERM<TAB>SCORE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scores are printed with six digits after the decimal point.

.SS query similar
Find indexed documents similar to
.I FILE
using cosine similarity over TF-IDF vectors.
.PP
.I FILE
must exactly match a path stored in the index. Use
.B query list
to inspect the stored paths.
.PP
The target document itself is excluded. Documents whose computed similarity is
zero are not printed.
.PP
The default result count is 10.
.PP
Output format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PATH<TAB>SCORE
.fi
.RE

.SS query search
Search indexed documents using a query string.
.PP
If
.I QUERY
is omitted, or if the first remaining argument is
.BR \-\-top ,
the query text is read from standard input. Leading and trailing whitespace is
removed.
.PP
When a query is supplied as an argument, only the first query argument is used.
A multi-word query must therefore be quoted by the shell:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query search "error handling rust" --top 20
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search reloads the current stopword file when the command runs. The index does
not store the stopword configuration used during
.BR save .
Changing the stopword file after building the index can therefore make search
query tokenization differ from indexing-time tokenization.
.PP
An empty query, including one containing only delimiters or configured
stopwords, produces no results. A textually empty query is reported as an
error.
.PP
Only documents with a positive cosine-similarity score are printed.
.PP
The default result count is 10.
.PP
Output format:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PATH<TAB>SCORE
.fi
.RE

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-index " PATH"
Use
.I PATH
as the index file.
.PP
For
.B save
the index is written to this path. For
.B query
the index is loaded from this path.
.PP
This option has precedence over
.B TFIDF_INDEX
and the default data-directory path.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
Limit the number of results for
.BR terms ,
.BR similar ,
or
.BR search .
.PP
The default is 20 for
.B terms
and 10 for
.B similar
and
.BR search .
.PP
If
.I N
cannot be parsed as a nonnegative platform-sized integer, the command default
is used. A value of zero produces no output.
.TP
.B \-\-
For
.BR save ,
stop processing command-line paths and read additional paths from standard
input.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage information to standard error and exit successfully.
.PP
These help operands are recognized only when they are the first top-level
argument. Subcommand-specific help operands are not implemented.

.SH INDEX PATH RESOLUTION
The index path is resolved in this order:
.IP "1." 4
The path supplied by
.BR \-\-index .
.IP "2." 4
The value of
.BR TFIDF_INDEX ,
if nonempty.
.IP "3." 4
The default index path:
.PP
.RS
.nf
DATA_DIRECTORY/tfidf.index
.fi
.RE

.SH DATA DIRECTORY RESOLUTION
The default data directory is resolved in this order:
.IP "1." 4
.BR TFIDF_DATA_DIR ,
if nonempty. The application name is not appended to this value.
.IP "2." 4
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME / TFIDF_APPNAME ,
if
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is nonempty.
.IP "3." 4
.BR HOME /.local/share/ TFIDF_APPNAME ,
if
.B HOME
is nonempty.
.IP "4." 4
.BR CURRENT_DIRECTORY / TFIDF_APPNAME ,
if no usable
.B HOME
value is available.
.PP
.B TFIDF_APPNAME
defaults to
.BR tfidf .

.SH CONFIGURATION FILES

.SS Stopwords
The first existing stopword file in the following order is loaded:
.IP "1." 4
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME / TFIDF_APPNAME /stopwords.txt
.IP "2." 4
.BR HOME /.config/ TFIDF_APPNAME /stopwords.txt
.PP
An unset or empty
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
or
.B HOME
does not contribute a candidate path.
.PP
The file is read one line at a time. Each line is trimmed and lowercased.
Nonempty lines are added as stopwords.
.PP
If no stopword file exists, an empty stopword set is used.
.PP
Stopwords are loaded independently during
.B save
and
.BR "query search" .
They are not embedded in the serialized index.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TFIDF_INDEX
Index file used when
.B \-\-index
is not supplied.
.TP
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
Explicit default data directory. When set, the default index path is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$TFIDF_DATA_DIR/tfidf.index
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B TFIDF_APPNAME
Application namespace used in XDG-style data and configuration paths. The
default value is
.BR tfidf .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Used to construct:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$XDG_DATA_HOME/$TFIDF_APPNAME/tfidf.index
.fi
.RE
.PP
when
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
is unset or empty.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Used to locate:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/$TFIDF_APPNAME/stopwords.txt
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B HOME
Used for the fallback data and stopword paths:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$HOME/.local/share/$TFIDF_APPNAME/tfidf.index
$HOME/.config/$TFIDF_APPNAME/stopwords.txt
.fi
.RE

.SH DEFAULTS
.TP
Application name
.B tfidf
.TP
Index filename
.B tfidf.index
.TP
.B query terms
result count
.B 20
.TP
.B query similar
result count
.B 10
.TP
.B query search
result count
.B 10
.TP
Stopwords
No stopwords are used if no configured stopword file exists.

.SH TOKENIZATION
Input is tokenized byte by byte.
.PP
A word token consists of:
.IP \(bu 2
ASCII letters;
.IP \(bu 2
ASCII digits;
.IP \(bu 2
underscore.
.PP
ASCII letters are lowercased. Every other byte is a token delimiter. The
tokenizer is ASCII-oriented; non-ASCII bytes delimit tokens.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Hello, world! test-case
.fi
.RE
.PP
is tokenized as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hello
world
test
case
.fi
.RE

.SH SCORING
For a term
.I t
and document
.IR d ,
term frequency is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
TF(t,d) = count(t,d) / |d|
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inverse document frequency is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
IDF(t) = ln((D + 1) / (df(t) + 1))
.fi
.RE
.PP
where
.I D
is the number of indexed documents and
.I df(t)
is the number of indexed documents containing the term.
.PP
The term score is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
TFIDF(t,d) = TF(t,d) * IDF(t)
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B similar
and
.B search
rank documents using cosine similarity:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cos(a,b) = (a . b) / (||a|| ||b||)
.fi
.RE
.PP
A zero vector has similarity zero. Terms occurring in every indexed document
receive an IDF of zero.

.SH OUTPUT
Normal query output is written to standard output without headers.
.PP
.B query list
prints:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PATH
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B query terms
prints:
.PP
.RS
.nf
TERM<TAB>SCORE
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B query similar
and
.B query search
print:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PATH<TAB>SCORE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Scores use fixed six-decimal formatting.
.PP
Usage messages and explicit command diagnostics are written to standard error.
Fatal errors returned by filesystem, I/O, or serialization operations are
reported by the Rust runtime.
.PP
A broken standard-output pipe causes successful termination without a
broken-pipe diagnostic.

.SH FILES
.TP
.B $TFIDF_DATA_DIR/tfidf.index
Default index when
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
is set.
.TP
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/$TFIDF_APPNAME/tfidf.index
Default index when
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
is unset and
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set.
.TP
.B $HOME/.local/share/$TFIDF_APPNAME/tfidf.index
Default index when neither
.B TFIDF_DATA_DIR
nor
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and
.B HOME
is available.
.TP
.B ./$TFIDF_APPNAME/tfidf.index
Fallback index when no usable
.B HOME
value is available.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/$TFIDF_APPNAME/stopwords.txt
Primary stopword file.
.TP
.B $HOME/.config/$TFIDF_APPNAME/stopwords.txt
Fallback stopword file.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including top-level help and termination caused by a closed output
pipe.
.TP
.B nonzero
Invalid command usage, missing save inputs, missing index, empty search query,
unknown command, filesystem failure, I/O failure, or serialization failure.

.SH EXAMPLES
Build an index using the default resolved path:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf save src/ notes/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build an index explicitly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf save --index docs.index src/ notes/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use the legacy positional index form:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf save docs.index src/ notes/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read input paths from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.nf
find src notes -type f | tfidf save --index docs.index --
.fi
.RE
.PP
Mix command-line and standard-input paths:
.PP
.RS
.nf
find notes -type f | tfidf save --index docs.index src/ --
.fi
.RE
.PP
List indexed documents:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query --index docs.index list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the top 100 terms:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query --index docs.index terms --top 100
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sort terms numerically by score:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query terms --top 100 | sort -k2,2nr
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find documents similar to an indexed file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query --index docs.index similar src/main.rs --top 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pass similar document paths to another command:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query similar src/main.rs | cut -f1 | xargs ${EDITOR:-vi}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search using a quoted query:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tfidf query search "error handling rust" --top 20
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read a search query from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo "memory management" | tfidf query search --top 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Select an index with the environment:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export TFIDF_INDEX="$PWD/docs.index"
tfidf save src/ notes/
tfidf query terms
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a separate application namespace:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export TFIDF_APPNAME=project-docs
mkdir -p "$HOME/.config/project-docs"
printf '%s\\n' the and of > "$HOME/.config/project-docs/stopwords.txt"
tfidf save "$HOME/work/docs"
.fi
.RE

.SH NOTES
Document paths are stored using their discovered string representation. They
are not canonicalized. Relative paths generally remain relative, and
.B query similar
requires an exact string match.
.PP
The index is serialized using
.BR bincode .
Its on-disk representation is an implementation detail and is not a stable
interchange format.
.PP
Saving replaces the complete index. Incremental updates, index merging, and
document removal are not exposed.
.PP
Filesystem traversal order, parallel file processing, and hash-map iteration
order are not specified. Do not rely on stable ordering between independently
built indexes, especially where scores are tied.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR awk (1),
.BR cut (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR xargs (1)
